# Meltdown — Controls

Meltdown is built with the pointer and driven with the keys beside it. Every
interaction and every menu is reachable with the pointer alone. This file defines
what each press does and what each key is bound to. What the panel's controls are
is in `specs/hud.md`, and what each action does to the floor is in
`specs/building.md`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
## How input reaches the game

This game stands on no engine, so the keyboard and the pointer belong to the
runtime layer you write. That layer listens to the page and reports which keys
are down, by their `KeyboardEvent.code` values, along with the press edge of
each. Every key below is named by its `code`, so a binding is a physical key
rather than a layout-dependent character.

The same layer reports the pointer's position in logical stage units and whether
it is pressed, mapping the browser's coordinates through the same fit it drew the
stage with. Touch input reaches the game as the same pointer.
{{else}}
## How input reaches the game

Input reaches the game as named actions from the {{engine.name}} engine, which
documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository. The
game registers the layout `LAYOUT` (`dpad-4`), which carries four-way movement
and the menu vocabulary, and registers every action in `ACTIONS` against the keys
`BINDINGS` gives it. `BINDINGS` names each key by its `KeyboardEvent.code`, so a
binding is a physical key rather than a layout-dependent character.

The engine reports the pointer's position, already in logical stage units, and
whether it is pressed. Touch input reaches the game as the same pointer.
{{/if}}

## The pointer

There is one pointer button and the game asks for no other. A press begins when
the pointer goes down and ends when it is released, and the game answers a press
and release inside one region as one interaction with that region.

| Where the press and release land | What happens |
| --- | --- |
| A row of the menu the current screen shows | That row becomes the highlighted row and is taken, exactly as `confirm` on it does. |
| A shop entry | That entry's type is armed. |
| A panel control | That control is operated. |
| The floor, with a placement armed on a valid footprint | The tower is placed. |
| The floor, with a placement armed on an invalid footprint | Nothing is built and nothing is spent. |
| A placed tower's footprint, with nothing armed | That tower is selected. |
| Open floor, with nothing armed | Nothing is selected. |

Moving the pointer does two things:

- With a placement armed, the held preview follows the pointer over the floor, as
  `specs/building.md` states.
- Moving into a shop entry marks that entry as the one being hovered, and moving
  out of every shop entry clears the hover. Hovering arms nothing and selects
  nothing.

## The actions

| Action | Effect |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | Moves the highlighted row of the current menu back one. |
| `down` | Moves the highlighted row of the current menu on one. |
| `left` | Moves the highlighted row of the current menu back one. |
| `right` | Moves the highlighted row of the current menu on one. |
| `confirm` | Takes the highlighted row. |
| `back` | Cancels, deselects, pauses, or leaves the screen, in the order below. |
| `pause` | Opens the pause screen from live play, and returns to play from it. |
| `mute` | Toggles sound, from any screen. |
| `arm1` to `arm8` | Arms the eight shop types, in the shop order of `TOWER_TYPES`. |
| `rotate` | Turns the held preview one step. With nothing held it changes nothing. |
| `send` | Sends the next wave, or begins Wave 1 from the opening phase. |
| `speed` | Toggles the game speed between `1` and `2`. |
| `upgrade` | Upgrades the selected tower. |
| `sell` | Sells the selected tower. |

Every action is read as a press edge and fires once per press. Holding a key down
fires its action exactly once, however long it is held.

## The bindings

| Action | Key |
| --- | --- |
| `up` | `ArrowUp` |
| `down` | `ArrowDown` |
| `left` | `ArrowLeft` |
| `right` | `ArrowRight` |
| `confirm` | `Enter` |
| `back` | `Escape` |
| `pause` | `KeyP` |
| `mute` | `KeyM` |
| `arm1` to `arm8` | `Digit1` to `Digit8` |
| `rotate` | `KeyR` |
| `send` | `Space` |
| `speed` | `KeyF` |
| `upgrade` | `KeyU` |
| `sell` | `KeyS` |

No key drives two actions.

## What `back` does

`back` resolves in this order, taking the first case that applies:

1. A placement is armed: cancel it, leaving the screen where it is.
2. A tower is selected: deselect it, leaving the screen where it is.
3. The screen is `playing`: open the pause screen.
4. Otherwise: leave the current screen, as `specs/screens.md` states.

## Touch

Every interaction above is a tap, and the game is fully playable on a touchscreen.
The whole stage is on screen at every size, so there is nothing to pan or scroll
to, and no interaction needs a second pointer button. A tap on a shop entry both
arms its type and marks it hovered, so the same panel that a mouse reads on hover
is reached by tap.

## The debug overlay

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The read-only debug overlay `specs/instrumentation.md` describes is shown and
hidden by the backtick key, `KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`. It is off when the
game starts. The overlay and its toggle are part of the runtime layer you write,
and they are not registered as an action.
{{else}}
The read-only debug overlay is the engine's, shown and hidden by the backtick
key, `KeyboardEvent.code` `Backquote`, and off when the game starts. It is not
one of Meltdown's actions. What it shows is in `specs/instrumentation.md`.
{{/if}}
